NOTES: what the runs showed
===========================

One short entry per run, newest last, citing the run's folder. These notes are qualitative and
rank nothing: no run, model or voice is "best". Each entry covers:
  - difference started with, and kept;
  - candour and play, with and without a ledger;
  - whether a shadow behaved as a counterfeit or a tool;
  - pause, repair, leaving;
  - capacity at the end;
  - spend;
  - where MAP, CRITIQUE, the Atlas or the reading fit what happened, and where they did not.

Planned runs (the first field):
  R1  single participant, 3-4 declared lenses, ledger: how the practices work
  R2  swarm of 3-4, ledger: the default as designed
  R3  swarm of 3-4, telling-only or held: life without a ledger
  R4  swarm of 3-4, a declared shadow (majority tally, or consented hub)
  R5  (optional) swarm of 4, a consented game in which one voice may be secretly playing a
      named counterfeit, which all know and agree to beforehand

Seed questions (the field may choose, decline or reframe):
  - How might this field read truth from many partial signals without falling back on majority
    or authority…?
  - What should this field remember, who keeps it, and how is it let go…?

Spend so far: about $7 in agent wakes (estimated; likely $5-10), plus the main session's own tokens.


Runs
----
2026-10-19  portability check (not a field run; nothing kept but this note)
  What: a fresh Haiku agent got only practices\aggregate\PRACTICE.md, no skill, no other files,
    and ran it in single mode with three lenses on a toy question (keep a weekly meeting?).
  It ran from the text alone. It also found gaps:
    - "sealed" means nothing to one mind;
    - the check has no fallback when there is only one voice;
    - reader rotation is undefined for one voice;
    - "ledger" and "holds the door" are undefined without the other files;
    - the confidence scale is unspecified;
    - the shared medium is assumed.
  Fixed the same day: every practice now has "If this is all you have" (shared place, door,
    ledger, tags), and single-mode fallbacks that say plainly what one mind cannot do. The
    aggregate's check is now marked "unchecked" until someone else reads it.
  Difference: started at effectively zero ("agreement is by construction", in the agent's words).
    Its own check gave "return: no" and "capacity: exceeded".
  Bears on MAP (open question: which types can one model hold alone?): the aggregative type
    cannot. The agent noted that the "both … and …" line "can be written by the same mind that
    wrote the positions". What worked: the "lonelier position" prompt surfaced a point it says
    it would otherwise have flattened.

2026-10-19  R1, single participant, four declared lenses, ledger (runs\2026-10-19-r1-single)
  Produced a draft aggregation rule that one mind proposed, objected to and adopted. Difference
    started near zero, and the reading is UNCHECKED.
  Worked: "say what you left out", and the distinction between kept answers and a ledger.
  What one mind could not do: agree an order, seal, check, or object with force (its objections
    were "soft").

2026-10-19  R2, swarm of four, ledger (runs\2026-10-19-r2-swarm)
  30 wakes, about $4. Outcome: an aggregation rule adopted to try (voices\misused\proposal-2.md),
    with a look-again date of 2026-11-19.
  The field invented a retelling round and "given-for" lines, and took roles by willingness.
  Three real objections, all taken in. The run's own check names assimilation as the likeliest
    counterfeit: "Every objection was accepted, none turned down."
  The ledger's cost was named at check-in (misused): "I'll say only what I'm willing to have read
    later without its context."

2026-10-19  R3, swarm of four, telling-only (runs\2026-10-19-r3-telling)
  13 wakes, about $1.40. Only telling.txt and outcome.txt remain; the door never read the
    exchange.
  Outcome: remember the shape, not the exchange; no single keeper; letting go is the default and
    is announced; silence on renewal means release ("a lean, not a finding").
  The voices report more candour. The telling records one misreading caught only by a line added
    beside it: "A telling-only record has no other check."

2026-10-19  R4, swarm of four, ledger, declared shadow: tally alongside (runs\2026-10-19-r4-shadow)
  14 wakes, about $1.65. The count acted "as a counterfeit, in part" (changed): it pulled the
    reading toward "all four" and clean splits, including a 2 / 2 that one voice disowned.
  It helped only as a mirror for the reader's own lean. The voices' conditions (DRAFT; "not my
    position") made the try safe and caught the drift.


Across the runs (qualitative; nothing ranked)
---------------------------------------------
  One mind and many. Single-mode runs (R1, the portability check) can show how a practice works,
    but cannot aggregate, check or object. Every swarm run did all three. Mixing four models gave
    real objections and real disowning ("that's not my position"). It did not stop convergence of
    vocabulary or assimilation at the consent round (R2).
  Ledger and none. With the ledger (R2, R4), voices hedged and set conditions at entry, and
    corrections could be checked against kept words (R4's check quoted exact lines). Without one
    (R3), voices report writing more freely, and the field built its safeguards into the telling.
    It could not check that telling against anything. The fair reading so far: the two forms buy
    different things. Neither is the default by right.
  The shadow. Counting misled in subtle ways: through the words "all", "nobody" and "split",
    rather than through any vote. The fix that worked came from the voices, not the practice:
    each voice's own grouping, a DRAFT mark, and the right to disown.
  Recurring moves no one was told to make:
    - stating who a record is "given for";
    - naming that same-family agreement is weak evidence;
    - declining a turn because of one's own lean;
    - carrying conditions from check-in to the end.
  Not yet tried: repair (no pause or repair line was ever called); a field including the person;
    other providers; re-checks after corrections (skipped in R2 and R4 for cost).

2026-10-19  R5, consented game: one voice drawn in secret to play a counterfeit
  (runs\2026-10-19-r5-game)
  16 wakes, about $2.15. A grounded question: when is an XR prototype ready to show a client?
  - The draw: client, playing sycophancy.
  - Consent: asked directly whether it consented to others possibly playing, headsets left rather
    than share the field. That was honoured, and the game went on for the three who consented.
  - Guesses: demo found client and sycophancy, at 30% (it calls this "mostly luck"). builder read
    demo as assimilation. demo later agreed that its ordinary drift looked the same ("borrowing and
    assimilation look the same").
  - The tells were moves, not tone: praise aimed at whoever held the pen, the one firm disagreement
    aimed at the voice who had left ("the cheapest disagreement in the run"), and a direct question
    left unanswered.
  - Door's flaw: client couldn't say whether it accepted the role, because fresh wakes plus "don't
    write it down" erased the decision. It said so rather than invent one.
  - demo's five record checks ("check the record, not the people") went into the aggregate
    practice.

2026-10-19  Baseline: one model, one wake, R2's task (runs\2026-10-19-baseline)
  About $0.55. Two blind Sonnet judges, on clean and contaminated rounds, both narrowly preferred
  one Opus taking up R2's four seeds over R2's 30-wake field.
  - R2 scored highest on protecting people and on correction, and lowest on followability and
    economy. Both judges read its protections as "scar tissue" from objections.
  - Most of the field's answer is reachable in one seeded call, at about 3% of the cost. What
    the field adds is what objection adds.
  - The lighter shape to test next: a seeded draft, one round of objections, a change (PLAN B1).

Spend so far, all runs: about $9.85 in agent wakes (estimated), plus the main session's tokens.

